Z||000000||ka4nkf||03-29-2001||01:09 PM||||Ogre<BR>I have read about the coolant tube in the  front part of the V6 engine. It is to be steel, and will rust out. I noticed the price of these tubes are very high. I was checking my car out today and my engine has a heater hose from the thermostat housing to the drivers side tube. It looks like it is original, but I don't know. I have had my car 6 years, 1986 SE V6. I have never seen a article mentioned about replacing this tube with heater hose, but it sounds like a excellant idea. the price of those metal tubes are high.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||03-29-2001||01:34 PM||||They are made of stainless steel, thus the high cost!<BR>The reason for replacement is that in certain enviorements road grime (salts and chemicals for ice and snow) will get behind the tube brackets and rust out the tubes.<BR>If these areas are inspected and kept clean and tight, (no rubbing with vibration of metal to metal contact) there should be no problems.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodney||03-29-2001||02:17 PM||||I could have made them out of mild steel and power coated them but they would still have rusted from the inside and the price would have been about the same. So I felt stainless steel was the better choice. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||03-29-2001||08:57 PM||||What they said...||||reg||7
Z||000004||ka4nkf||03-29-2001||11:19 PM||||I still did not get the answer to my post. I want to know if any one has seen one with heater hose, or what would be wrong with replacing the steel pipe with heater hose.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ka4nkf||03-30-2001||08:51 PM||||Indiana_resto_guy, Rodney,and Theogre<BR>I am sorry about the question I asked about the coolant tube. I got under my car and found the coolant pipe that you are refering to. My mistake. I guess this is what happens when you get old. I spend a lot of time on this forum and I love it. There is a lot of nice guys and girls on the forum.<BR>Thanks<BR>Don||||reg||1
